  "accuracyFacts": [
    "Integral-to-integral explicit conversions in checked context throw OverflowException when out of range; unchecked integral conversions follow language-defined truncation/extension rules.",
    "Floating-point-to-integral conversions round toward zero for in-range values. In checked context an out-of-range conversion throws; unchecked out-of-range results are unspecified by C# language rules, even where a .NET runtime documents saturation.",
    "Decimal-to-integral conversions round toward zero and always throw OverflowException for out-of-range results, regardless of checked or unchecked context.",
    "Do not generalize .NET runtime saturation across targets: .NET 9 documents unchecked float/double-to-int/uint/long/ulong behavior on specified runtimes; .NET 11 adds unchecked conversions to sbyte/byte/short/ushort/char on CoreCLR and Native AOT, excluding Mono. Checked conversions still throw for out-of-range inputs.",
    "In .NET 11, decimal-to-float/double conversions are correctly rounded from the exact source value; this does not mean they preserve all decimal precision."
  ]
